lain":"Themes in a Shakespeare play are complex claims supported across acts, often echoed by motifs (repeated images or situations). Avoid single-slogan morals; show tension between values the play stages.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Reducing the play to ''love conquers all'' without counter-evidence. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Play themes.","caption":"Show value tension.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Theme</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">complex claim</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Motif</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">echoed detail</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Theme claim: loyalty is tested when private love conflicts with public duty. Motif: letters/messages misdelivered. Evide Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Theme claim: loyalty is tested when private love conflicts with public duty. Motif: letters/messages misdelivered. Evide Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does","A guess with no explanation","A claim unrelated to the text"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about play theme with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nTheme claim: loyalty is tested when private love conflicts with public duty. Motif: letters/messages misdelivered. Evidence from two acts. Think about how this word or relationship creates meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUnderline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, ma